## Why This Is Asked

This question probes data governance, access control, and incident response fundamentals for beginner CISSP candidates, with a realistic data-import scenario.

## Key Concepts

- Data classification and handling
- RBAC/ABAC and just-in-time access
- Data provenance and SBOM concepts
- Encryption at rest and in transit; KMS/key rotation
- Immutable audit logging and incident response/testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Validate payload schema and attach a signed attestation
function validateAndSign(payload, signer) {
  // simple schema check
  if (!payload || !payload.data) throw new Error('Invalid');
  const attestation = signer.sign(JSON.stringify(payload));
  return { payload, attestation };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test end-to-end import validation in CI/CD?
- How should failed attestations be handled and remediated?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crypto agility, cross-cloud governance, and practical CISSP controls for data at rest and in transit.

## Key Concepts
- Crypto agility and KMS key hierarchies across clouds
- Envelope encryption, per-tenant DEKs, field-level encryption
- Key rotation, revocation, auditability, incident response
- Data residency and edge deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rotation workflow
rotateMasterKey(cloud, keyId) {
  wrapNewDEKWithMasterKey(keyId);
  rewrapAllDataKeys();
  validateNoDowntime();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test key rotation impact on latency and availability?
- What would you monitor to detect key-compromise attempts and how would you respond?
